The verdict asks whether production named the CALLING FUNCTION in a chain's
io_edge_src. The previous revision called the residual bucket "UNREACHABLE",
which reads as "hypergumbo cannot see this primitive" -- and that overstates it.
All five residuals DO produce a chain, anchored to
`python:<external>:0-0:file:external_symbol`, a non-caller name. "Sees the
boundary, cannot say who called it" is a different defect with a different owner
(WI-pimun) than "cannot see the boundary", and folding them together inflates a
gap number with someone else's bug.

The run now prints the io_edge_src KIND-slot tally and the count of primitives
anchored to a non-caller name, alongside the verdicts.

A SLOT-READING ERROR OF MY OWN, fixed and documented in the file because it
nearly shipped as a retraction. An attributed chain reads
`python:probe.py:0-0:<probe_fn>:external_symbol` -- the caller sits in the NAME
slot while the KIND slot says `external_symbol` for EVERY chain on this fixture.
Counting the kind slot and calling it "the anchor" made two correct readings look
contradictory, and I concluded the instrument was non-reproducible; two
back-to-back runs on separate workdirs then returned identical tables
(153/16/41/5). The instrument was fine; the reading was wrong. `entry_points` is
likewise empty on all 476 chains here, so it cannot discriminate either and is no
longer used as the signal -- the discriminator is now the NAME slot.

VERDICTS ARE UNCHANGED by this commit (153/16/41/5), which is the point: the
change is disclosure and vocabulary, not behaviour.

WI-pimun gains a five-function reproducer: subprocess.Popen anchors to
`python:app.py:1-1:file:file` with entry_points=[] while subprocess.run four
lines away anchors to its caller. Two Popen call sites collapse to ONE
file-anchored chain, so the callers are unrecoverable from the output -- for the
boundary most likely to matter in a safety claim.

